*The following was reported by OpenAI via email:*

- **Finding:** Use-after-free and permit leak
- **Affected Software:** Tokio 1.53.1 and development snapshot `eb9cdf2ff012` (experimental semaphore tracing)
- **Date:** September 2026
- **Discoverer:** OpenAI (OutboundDisclosures@openai.com)

A recoverable tracing-subscriber panic during permit assignment leaves a completed waiter in the semaphore list. The pending acquisition can then complete and free its allocation without unlinking it; a later release dereferences the freed waiter. This requires `tokio_unstable`, Tokio tracing, and a subscriber that unwinds. No affected standard subscriber has been identified.

## Reproduction

Queue an acquisition, arm a one-shot subscriber panic, then catch the panic from `add_permits`. Complete and free the acquisition normally before releasing another permit. The control disables only the subscriber panic.

Use a standalone PoC directory beside an existing `tokio/` checkout at `eb9cdf2ff012ec22d4efd74cf46d04222264cd8e`. Save the files below at the shown paths and run commands from the PoC directory.

`Cargo.toml`:

```toml
[package]
name = "tokio-sync-audit-poc"
version = "0.0.0"
edition = "2024"

[workspace]

[dependencies]
tokio = { path = "../tokio/tokio", features = ["sync", "tracing", "rt"] }
tracing = "=0.1.44"
```

`src/lib.rs`:

```rust
#![forbid(unsafe_code)]

use std::sync::Arc;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};
use tracing::span::{Attributes, Id, Record};
use tracing::{Event, Metadata, Subscriber};

pub struct PanicSubscriber(pub Arc<AtomicBool>);

impl Subscriber for PanicSubscriber {
    fn enabled(&self, _: &Metadata<'_>) -> bool {
        true
    }

    fn new_span(&self, _: &Attributes<'_>) -> Id {
        Id::from_u64(1)
    }

    fn record(&self, _: &Id, _: &Record<'_>) {}

    fn record_follows_from(&self, _: &Id, _: &Id) {}

    fn event(&self, event: &Event<'_>) {
        // Panic once, only after the semaphore reports assigning permits.
        if event
            .metadata()
            .fields()
            .field("permits_obtained")
            .is_some()
            && self.0.swap(false, Ordering::SeqCst)
        {
            panic!("controlled safe Subscriber panic after permit assignment");
        }
    }

    fn enter(&self, _: &Id) {}

    fn exit(&self, _: &Id) {}
}
```

`src/main.rs`:

```rust
#![forbid(unsafe_code)]

use std::future::Future;
use std::panic::{AssertUnwindSafe, catch_unwind};
use std::sync::Arc;
use std::sync::atomic::{AtomicBool, Ordering};
use std::task::{Context, Poll, Waker};
use tokio::sync::Semaphore;
use tokio_sync_audit_poc::PanicSubscriber;

fn main() {
    let control = std::env::args().any(|arg| arg == "--control");
    let armed = Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(false));

    tracing::subscriber::with_default(PanicSubscriber(armed.clone()), || {
        // Poll once to link the waiting acquisition before enabling the panic.
        let semaphore = Semaphore::new(0);
        let mut acquire = Box::pin(semaphore.acquire());
        let mut context = Context::from_waker(Waker::noop());
        assert!(acquire.as_mut().poll(&mut context).is_pending());

        // Recover from the release callback; the acquisition itself has not panicked.
        armed.store(!control, Ordering::SeqCst);
        let result = catch_unwind(AssertUnwindSafe(|| semaphore.add_permits(1)));
        assert_eq!(result.is_err(), !control);

        eprintln!(
            "release returned (panicked = {}); polling the acquisition",
            result.is_err()
        );
        let permit = match acquire.as_mut().poll(&mut context) {
            Poll::Ready(Ok(permit)) => permit,
            _ => panic!("the fully assigned acquisition must be ready"),
        };

        // In the trigger, freeing the completed future leaves its stale list entry behind.
        drop(acquire);

        eprintln!("acquisition allocation freed; releasing another permit");
        semaphore.add_permits(1);

        drop(permit);
    });
}
```

